# The test runs on the phone, not in an emulator

The factory hands the owner a **finished product**, so testing is not a stage that can be outsourced to
the reviewer. The agent that built the app runs it. The owner's instruction, recorded 2026-10-01:

> "Есть такой очень важный пункт, как тестирование, потому что агент должен сам себя полностью всё
> протестировать, так как он уже отдаёт полностью готовленный нам продукт. Суть в том, что он должен
> тестировать не на эмуляторе, потому что эмулятор нагружает систему, становится всё медленно и всё
> плохо. Я подключаю почти всегда через АДБ полностью доступный телефон для всего управления Samsung
> Galaxy A16. Приоритет отдавать ему, пускай всё тестирует на нём. Там и тапы, и свачи, и всё, короче,
> и качать можно, и устанавливать можно. Поэтому это полностью телефон для тестирования, может
> спокойно им управлять."

In one sentence: **the attached Samsung Galaxy A16 over adb is the test target, it is fully ours to
drive, and an emulator is not used** — because a running AVD loads the machine, and a slow machine
slows every later slot in the same day. The emulator is not a cheaper device; it is a tax on the whole
factory.

## The measured starting position

- `scripts/media-capture.mjs` already speaks to a device rather than to a machine: it finds `adb` on
  `PATH` or under `$ANDROID_HOME/platform-tools` (`findAdb()`), lists devices with `adb devices`,
  addresses the chosen one as `adb -s <serial> …`, installs the release APK
  (`adb install -r app/build/outputs/apk/release/app-release.apk` is the remedy its own error prints),
  launches with `monkey -p <applicationId> -c android.intent.category.LAUNCHER 1`, reads the screen with
  `exec-out uiautomator dump /dev/tty` and `dumpsys package`, drives `shell input tap` / `input text` /
  `input keyevent`, and photographs with `exec-out screencap -p` as **bytes**.
- That is the whole controllable surface this rule relies on. Nothing here is invented: every action
  the rule asks for is a subcommand the factory already runs, or a `DroidAgentKit` tool already
  configured for this project in `.cursor/mcp.json`.
- Two pieces of existing text still point at an emulator, and this rule supersedes both: the hint
  `emulatorHint()` prints when no device is attached, and `.cursor/commands/ui-verify.md`'s table,
  which still lists "Android emulator + adb + screenshot — Preferred for Compose UI". Correcting them
  is the follow-up; reading them is not permission.

## Rules

- **The test target is a device in `device` state on `adb devices`, and with more than one attached
  every command is addressed by `-s <serial>`.** A serial that shows `offline` is not a target — the
  device-capture readiness probe measured exactly that state and refused it. `DroidAgentKit`'s
  `android_devices_list` is the same reading through the MCP server.
- **The phone is what the app is run on; an emulator is not a substitute.** No AVD is started to
  make a rule green. If the phone is not attached, the run is `UNVERIFIED` (below) and never a pass
  bought with an emulator.
- **The device is fully controllable and the test is expected to use it.** Install
  (`android_app_install`, or `adb install -r <apk>`), launch (`android_app_launch`), tap and swipe
  (`shell input tap` / `input swipe`, or `android_ui_find` for the point), type (`shell input text`),
  go back (`shell input keyevent 4`), photograph (`android_screen_snapshot` /
  `exec-out screencap -p`), read the hierarchy (`android_accessibility_snapshot` /
  `exec-out uiautomator dump`), read the log (`android_logcat_capture`), triage a crash
  (`android_crash_triage`). "I could not reach that screen" is a claim about the app or about the run,
  not a limit of the phone.
- **A tap coordinate is read, not guessed.** `android_ui_find` returns the tap point for a text,
  content description or resource id and reports ambiguous matches as candidates instead of picking
  one. Hand-eyeballing a pixel offset from a screenshot is how a journey silently exercises the wrong
  control and still produces six green-looking captures.
- **The artifact under test is the artifact the owner is handed.** In development that is the debug
  APK built from the revision under review; in release it is the signed artifact, and
  `media-capture.mjs` already refuses to publish anything until the **installed** `base.apk` is
  byte-identical to the recorded one — measured 2026-09-25, a stale install
  (`installed sha256=075224ba…` against the rebuilt `f8706756…`) was refused with exit 2 and no
  publication. A screen photographed on an older install is evidence about a different app, and it
  must not be reported as evidence about this one.
- **Reading the source is not a test.** A behaviour claim needs a run that observed it: the app
  launched, the screen reached, the string rendered, the value stored, the crash absent. A code path
  that looks correct and was never executed is `UNVERIFIED` — the same standard
  `.cursor/rules/verification-discipline.mdc` holds every other claim to, applied to the app itself.
- **No device attached means `UNVERIFIED`, and it is reported as such.** Not `PASS`, not "works as
  far as we can tell", and above all not an emulator started quietly to turn the row green. Say which
  serial was missing, say what remains unrun, and hand the owner the list — an honest gap is a
  question he can answer in one sentence.
- **Gradle stays one process per slot, so device runs serialize with builds.** The app must be built
  before it can be installed: one Gradle invocation produces the APK
  (`.cursor/rules/build-speed.mdc`), and the device run happens after it, in the same slot, alone.
  A device being attached does not license a second concurrent build, and a build started while a run
  is installing the previous APK corrupts the state the run is reading.
- **The phone is shared with the owner, and the run is a guest on it.** A test may install its own
  package, launch it, clear **its own** applicationId's data (`shell pm clear <applicationId>` is the
  permitted shape — it is the app under test), and change a per-app setting
  (`shell cmd locale set-app-locales <applicationId> --locales <locale>`). It may not factory-reset
  the device, may not `pm uninstall` a package it did not install, may not clear another app's data,
  and may not leave a device-wide setting changed: an IME the run disabled is re-enabled, a locale a
  run set is restored. The owner's phone is where his own work lives.
- **Every captured file goes under `.kiln/scratch/`, never the system temp directory.** Screenshots,
  `uiautomator` dumps and logcat captures are written through `scripts/lib/scratch.mjs`
  (`SCRATCH_DIR`, `scratchDirFor('device-<slot>')`) — not `%TEMP%`/`%TMP%`, not `$TMPDIR`, not
  anywhere else outside the repository. `.cursor/rules/workspace-hygiene.mdc` governs this and the
  reason is the owner's machine: a factory that fills the system temp directory has spent something
  that was not its to spend, and a PNG among the files makes that mess unreadable rather than small.
- **A capture is bytes, and on Windows it is never piped as text.** `adb exec-out screencap -p`
  through a text pipeline mangles CR/LF and yields a file that is not a PNG — the device-capture
  readiness probe recorded the trap, and `media-capture.mjs` passes `{ binary: true }` for exactly
  this reason.

## What is banned

- Starting an AVD to satisfy a test row, and any report that says "tested on the emulator" as though
  the target were a choice.
- A `PASS` for app behaviour whose only evidence is the source file, the diff, or the compile.
- A silent substitution of one artifact for another: a journey replayed against a previous install,
  a debug APK offered as the release evidence, a screenshot from an older run.
- Reporting a UI journey as verified while the taps were hand-guessed from coordinates nobody read
  from the hierarchy.
- Making a second Gradle invocation concurrent with an install or a run in the same slot.
- Wiping, resetting or uninstalling anything on the owner's phone that the factory did not put there,
  and leaving a device-wide setting changed behind a run.
- Writing a screenshot, a UI dump or a log to the system temp directory because it was the convenient
  path.
- Turning "no device is attached" into a pass by any route, including narrowing the claim until the
  run becomes unnecessary.

## How it is verified

This rule is enforced by **the run and its record** — and, since 2026-10-02, by a sensor that reads
that record rather than by prose alone. `slot.device` in `scripts/core/device.mjs` requires
`apps/<slot>/device-verification.json` to name the serial, the timestamp, the artifact path and its
sha256, and at least three observations of what was tapped and what appeared; when the built
`app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk` exists it **recomputes that hash and fails on a
disagreement**, which is `scripts/lib/apk-provenance.mjs`'s rule applied at the point it was missing.
A slot carrying no record is `UNVERIFIED`, never a pass.

What the sensor cannot do, stated rather than implied: it proves that **a record exists and agrees
with the artifact**. It cannot prove the run happened, that the taps were real, or that an
observation describes what was on the screen — a fabricated record passes it. So the record is still
the evidence a later reader contradicts, and this is what a handoff must carry:

```text
adb devices -l                              # the attached serial, in `device` state
<DroidAgentKit android_devices_list>        # the same reading through the MCP server
<DroidAgentKit android_app_install / android_app_launch>
<DroidAgentKit android_ui_find>             # the tap point, read from the hierarchy
<DroidAgentKit android_screen_snapshot>     # what was on the screen
<DroidAgentKit android_logcat_capture / android_crash_triage>
<DroidAgentKit android_test_run>            # an allowlisted test task, with its JUnit XML parsed
node scripts/media-capture.mjs --slot <slug>  # phase 2: refuses a stale install, five-link chain
node scripts/verify.mjs --slot <slug> --build # the slot's source checks + assembleDebug
```

- The record names the **serial**, the artifact and its hash, the revision, and what was observed on
  screen — the observation that would have made the claim negative is stated before the run, as
  `harness/roles/verifier.md` requires of every verdict. A "tested, works" with no serial and no
  artifact is prose.
- `media-capture.mjs` is the one place where this rule has teeth in code, and only in phase 2: its
  provenance chain walks HEAD → `dist/release-manifest.json` → the recorded sha256 → the APK on disk →
  the installed `base.apk`, and a missing or mismatched link is fatal
  (`.cursor/rules/release-gate.mdc`; `scripts/lib/apk-provenance.mjs` carries the offline suite).
- Phase 1 has no such script. Its device evidence is the run the builder performed on the debug APK
  from the revision being handed over, and it belongs in the handoff beside the build that produced
  it — the same revision the owner installs at checkpoint 1.5, so a claim about the app and the APK he
  is holding are the same sentence.
- A device that is **not** attached produces the `UNVERIFIED` row, printed where the test would have
  been, with the reason. That row is the check: a rule that reports a gap is worth more than a rule
  that reports a pass nobody ran.
